**Production Performance Tuning: Resolving High Swap Utilization by Optimizing Linux Swappiness**

## Overview

A production customer reported unexpectedly high swap memory utilization on multiple Linux servers, despite the systems having sufficient available 
RAM. The concern was that excessive swap usage might impact application performance and indicate a potential memory leak.

An investigation was performed across the affected servers to analyze memory consumption, kernel memory management, and swap behavior. While no 
memory exhaustion or application issues were identified, differences in the Linux **swappiness** configuration explained the inconsistent swap 
utilization between servers.

By tuning the swappiness value to align with production standards, unnecessary swap usage was eliminated without requiring any hardware upgrades or 
application changes.

---

## Environment

| Component         | Details                                        |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| Operating System  | Red Hat Enterprise Linux                       |
| Environment       | Production                                     |
| Affected Servers  | munmspdata5, munmspdata6, munmspbe1, munmspbe2 |
| Memory Management | Linux Virtual Memory                           |
| Kernel Parameter  | vm.swappiness                                  |

---

## Problem Statement

The customer observed high swap utilization on multiple production servers and requested an investigation into why swap was being consumed even 
though sufficient physical memory appeared to be available.

Memory utilization varied across servers, but two servers had nearly **100% swap utilization** despite relatively low RAM consumption.

---

## Requirements

* Investigate the cause of high swap utilization.
* Verify whether memory exhaustion existed.
* Compare memory management across affected servers.
* Identify configuration differences.
* Recommend an optimized solution.

---

## Investigation

### Step 1 – Verify Memory Utilization

Collected memory statistics using:

```bash
free -h
```

The analysis showed that all servers still had sufficient physical memory available.

Example:

| Server      | RAM Used | Available Memory | Swap Usage |
| ----------- | -------- | ---------------- | ---------- |
| munmspbe1   | ~63%     | 13 GB            | 1.7 GB     |
| munmspbe2   | ~63%     | 13 GB            | 2 GB       |
| munmspdata5 | ~38%     | 14 GB            | 11 GB      |
| munmspdata6 | ~38%     | 14 GB            | 11 GB      |

At first glance, the Data servers appeared abnormal because swap was fully utilized while more than half of the physical memory remained available.

---

### Step 2 – Verify System Load

Reviewed:

* CPU utilization
* Load average
* Running processes
* Memory pressure
* Application performance

No abnormal resource utilization or memory-intensive processes were observed.

The operating system remained stable throughout the investigation.

---

### Step 3 – Compare Kernel Memory Configuration

Compared the Linux virtual memory settings across all affected servers.

Checked:

```bash
cat /proc/sys/vm/swappiness
```

Results:

| Server      | Swappiness |
| ----------- | ---------- |
| munmspbe1   | 30         |
| munmspbe2   | 30         |
| munmspdata5 | 60         |
| munmspdata6 | 60         |

This configuration difference immediately stood out.

---

### Step 4 – Analyze Swap Behaviour

Further analysis showed:

* BE servers used swap minimally despite higher RAM utilization.
* Data servers aggressively moved inactive pages to swap even though sufficient RAM remained available.

No evidence suggested:

* Memory leaks
* Out-of-memory conditions
* Kernel memory exhaustion
* Application memory issues

The observed behavior was consistent with Linux virtual memory management using a higher swappiness value.

---

## Root Cause

The affected Data servers were configured with a **swappiness value of 60**, while the BE servers used **30**.

A higher swappiness value causes the Linux kernel to move inactive memory pages to swap more aggressively, even when adequate physical memory is 
still available.

As a result:

* RAM utilization remained relatively low.
* Swap usage gradually increased until it reached 100%.
* Applications continued operating normally, but the high swap utilization created concern and could potentially impact performance over time.

The issue was therefore configuration-related rather than a hardware or application problem.

---

## Resolution

To align the servers with the production standard, the swappiness value was reduced.

Current value:

```bash
cat /proc/sys/vm/swappiness
```

Applied the updated configuration:

```bash
sysctl -w vm.swappiness=30
```

Persisted the configuration:

```text
vm.swappiness = 30
```

Reloaded the kernel parameters.

```bash
sysctl --system
```

---

## Validation

After applying the new swappiness value:

Verified:

* Reduced swap activity
* Stable memory utilization
* Healthy application performance
* No increase in CPU or memory pressure
* No abnormal kernel events

Observed behaviour:

* RAM usage increased as expected.
* Swap utilization gradually reduced.
* Servers continued operating normally.

---

## Outcome

* Successfully identified that high swap utilization was caused by kernel memory management configuration.
* Standardized the swappiness value across production servers.
* Reduced unnecessary swap consumption.
* Improved memory utilization efficiency.
* No application downtime or hardware upgrades were required.

